const articleHtml = "<p>A parrot can look bright, eat well, and still be carrying a pathogen that matters to the birds it will live with. That is why screening before purchase or breeding is less about finding a single \"sick bird\" and more about reducing the chance of introducing an infection into a collection.</p>\n<p>This guide covers the tests most often discussed for parrots, what they can and cannot tell you, and how to make a sensible decision without treating a laboratory result as a guarantee.</p>\n<h2>Why Parrots Need Pre-Purchase Screening</h2>\n<p>Parrots are often moved between breeders, dealers, pet shops, and private homes. Each move creates a new contact network. A young bird may have been recently weaned, transported, or mixed with birds from several sources.</p>\n<p>The first question is not \"which panel should I order?\" It is \"what am I trying to prevent?\" For a single pet, the concern may be a clinically significant infection. For a breeder, the concern may be introducing a pathogen into a breeding population. Those are different risk calculations.</p>\n<h2>Common Diseases Considered in Parrot Screening</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Target</th>\n<th>What the test looks for</th>\n<th>Main limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>PBFD / BFDV</td>\n<td>Beak and feather disease virus nucleic acid</td>\n<td>A negative sample does not prove the bird will never become infected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Avian polyomavirus (APV)</td>\n<td>APV nucleic acid</td>\n<td>Detection depends on sample, timing, and assay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Chlamydia psittaci</em></td>\n<td>Bacterial nucleic acid</td>\n<td>Intermittent shedding can produce a negative result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other targets</td>\n<td>Selected bacteria, fungi, or viruses depending on the clinical question</td>\n<td>A panel is not a complete list of all parrot pathogens</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The current laboratory menu and sample requirements should be confirmed before ordering. A test name is not a promise that every strain or presentation will be detected.</p>\n<h2>PBFD Testing: What Buyers and Breeders Should Know</h2>\n<p>Psittacine beak and feather disease is associated with a circovirus that can affect feathers, beak, and immune function. Diagnosis is not made by appearance alone; a bird can be infected without the classic feather changes at the time of examination.</p>\n<p>PCR testing of an appropriate sample, often a growing feather follicle or a swab depending on the assay, can detect viral nucleic acid. The result needs to be read with the bird's age, history, and clinical signs.</p>\n<p>A negative result is reassuring for the sample tested, not a lifetime insurance policy. If the bird is young, recently exposed, or from a high-risk source, a veterinarian may recommend repeat testing or continued observation.</p>\n<h2>Avian Polyomavirus Testing</h2>\n<p>Avian polyomavirus can cause disease in young psittacines and can be carried by older birds. Testing is most useful when it is part of a broader health assessment, not as a standalone clearance certificate.</p>\n<p>The same principles apply:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the sample must be appropriate for the assay;</li>\n<li>the timing must match the exposure history;</li>\n<li>a negative result does not exclude infection with every pathogen;</li>\n<li>a positive result needs clinical and epidemiological context.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Chlamydia psittaci and Zoonotic Considerations</h2>\n<p><em>Chlamydia psittaci</em> is a bacterium that can infect birds and can also cause illness in people who have close contact with infected birds or contaminated material.</p>\n<p>For that reason, a positive or suspected result is not only a bird-management issue. It may require veterinary advice, hygiene measures, and, where relevant, public-health guidance.</p>\n<p>Testing can support a diagnosis, but it does not replace a veterinary assessment or a risk review of the household, aviary, or breeding facility.</p>\n<h2>Veterinary Examination vs PCR Testing</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Assessment</th>\n<th>What it adds</th>\n<th>What it does not replace</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Veterinary examination</td>\n<td>Body condition, lesions, respiratory and gastrointestinal signs</td>\n<td>Laboratory evidence of a specific pathogen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PCR / qPCR</td>\n<td>Detection of a defined nucleic-acid target</td>\n<td>Clinical judgement and quarantine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>History review</td>\n<td>Source, age, mixing, transport, previous illness</td>\n<td>Current infection status</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The best use of testing is alongside an examination, not instead of one.</p>\n<h2>Screening Individual Parrots Before Purchase</h2>\n<p>For a single pet bird, a sensible pre-purchase conversation includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the bird's age and origin;</li>\n<li>whether it has been mixed with other birds;</li>\n<li>any previous illness or treatment;</li>\n<li>a physical examination by a veterinarian;</li>\n<li>targeted testing where the risk justifies it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A seller's assurance is not a laboratory result. Ask what was tested, which sample was used, when it was collected, and whether the report belongs to that bird.</p>\n<h2>Screening Breeding Pairs Before Mating</h2>\n<p>Breeders have a different stake: an infected bird can affect a mate, offspring, and the reputation of a breeding program.</p>\n<p>A pre-breeding review may include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a physical examination of both birds;</li>\n<li>a review of previous breeding performance;</li>\n<li>targeted testing for pathogens relevant to the species and the facility;</li>\n<li>a plan for new birds entering the breeding group.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not every pair needs every test. The right plan depends on the species, the source of the birds, and the history of the facility.</p>\n<h2>Testing Young Birds and Newly Weaned Parrots</h2>\n<p>Young birds are not simply small adults. Maternal antibody, recent weaning, transport stress, and the timing of exposure can all affect what a test shows.</p>\n<p>A negative result in a recently exposed young bird may reflect testing too early. A positive result may reflect transient carriage or contamination. The laboratory result should be interpreted by someone who knows the bird's history.</p>\n<h2>Interpreting Positive and Negative Results</h2>\n<p>A positive PCR result means the target was detected in the sample. It does not automatically mean the bird has clinical disease, and it does not tell you how severe the infection will be.</p>\n<p>A negative result means the target was not detected in that sample. It does not prove the bird is free of infection, especially when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the sample was collected too early;</li>\n<li>the pathogen is shed intermittently;</li>\n<li>the sample type was not optimal;</li>\n<li>the assay does not cover the strain present.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An inconclusive or invalid result should not be treated as a negative. It usually means the laboratory could not produce a reliable answer from that sample.</p>\n<h2>The Limits of a Single Test</h2>\n<p>A single test answers a narrow question. It does not answer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>whether the bird will become ill later;</li>\n<li>whether the bird is safe for every other bird;</li>\n<li>whether the aviary is free of infection;</li>\n<li>whether the seller's entire collection is healthy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Screening is one part of a biosecurity plan that also includes quarantine, cleaning, traffic control, and record keeping.</p>\n<h2>Scenario: An African grey with no testing history</h2>\n<p>A buyer is offered a hand-raised African grey with no testing history. The bird is alert, fully feathered, and eating, and the seller says it has never been ill. The buyer has another parrot at home and can keep the grey in a separate room. The uncertainty is its earlier exposure and the timing of any infection: PBFD can be present without obvious plumage signs, and polyomavirus concerns depend on age, contacts, and sample timing.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ask for hatching date, rearing history, contacts, and previous samples, and pause the purchase while these are reviewed. Screening cannot make an untraceable history complete.</li>\n<li>Arrange a pre-purchase veterinary examination and discuss PBFD/BFDV, polyomavirus, and <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em> where relevant. Let the veterinarian and laboratory choose feather, blood, or swab samples.</li>\n<li>Review how to <a href=\"/how-to-interpret-bird-disease-screening-panel/\">interpret a screening panel</a> before collection, then quarantine the grey rather than placing it beside the resident parrot.</li>\n<li>If Not Detected, continue observation and ask when a repeat sample is justified. The result does not promise the bird will remain free of infection.</li>\n<li>If Detected or Inconclusive, keep separation, obtain veterinary follow-up, and consider repeat or confirmatory testing. The purchase, rehoming, or treatment is not decided by one report.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Result</th><th>What changes</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Not Detected</td><td>Continue quarantine; ask whether timing or history warrants a repeat.</td><td>The grey is disease-free or safe to mix with the resident parrot.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Detected</td><td>Keep separation and obtain veterinary interpretation and follow-up sampling.</td><td>The bird is ill, the exposure source, or the right treatment.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inconclusive or Invalid</td><td>Do not treat it as negative; arrange a new sample or method as advised.</td><td>Presence or absence of the target.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What we would do:</strong> We would pause the purchase until the grey has been examined and sampled, because the buyer cannot reconstruct its exposure history from the seller's account. The wider <a href=\"/bird-health-screening-biosecurity/\">pre-purchase screening and biosecurity plan</a> should guide quarantine even if the first result is favorable. This advice would not apply if the bird were showing severe or worsening signs, or if the household included someone at higher risk from psittacosis; veterinary advice should then come first.</p>\n<p>This is a testing-strategy example, not a diagnosis or treatment plan, and a veterinarian must interpret results with clinical findings.</p>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO provides avian molecular testing for selected parrot pathogens, including PBFD/BFDV, avian polyomavirus, and <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em> where the current menu supports the sample and target. This article is a testing-strategy guide, not a veterinary diagnosis or a guarantee that a bird is free of infection. The current target list and sample requirements should be confirmed with the laboratory and the bird's veterinarian.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Is a negative PBFD test enough to buy a parrot?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A negative result applies to the sample and target tested. Ask when the sample was taken, what was tested, and whether the bird's history warrants repeat testing or quarantine.</p>\n<p><strong>Can a parrot have PBFD and look normal?</strong></p>\n<p>Yes. Clinical signs can be absent or subtle, especially early. A physical examination and laboratory testing answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Should I test for Chlamydia psittaci before buying a parrot?</strong></p>\n<p>It is worth discussing with a veterinarian, especially if the bird has respiratory signs, came from a high-risk source, or will live with people at higher risk. Testing is not a substitute for hygiene and veterinary advice.</p>\n<p><strong>Does a positive result mean the bird must be euthanized?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A laboratory result is not a treatment or euthanasia decision. A veterinarian should interpret it with the bird's condition, the pathogen, and the risks to other birds and people.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I test a bird myself at home?</strong></p>\n<p>Sample collection may be possible, but the value of a molecular test depends on the right sample, timing, transport, and laboratory quality control. A home test kit is not equivalent to a validated laboratory workflow.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Pet birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds</li>\n<li>CDC — Psittacosis: About. https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>CDC — Bird flu: About. https://www.cdc.gov/bird-flu/about/index.html</li>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock: Biosecurity. https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>WOAH — Avian influenza. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/avian-influenza/</li>\n<li>AAVLD — Accreditation requirements. https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n</ol>";
const faqJsonLd = {"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Is a negative PBFD test enough to buy a parrot?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. A negative result applies to the sample and target tested. Ask when the sample was taken, what was tested, and whether the bird's history warrants repeat testing or quarantine."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can a parrot have PBFD and look normal?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Clinical signs can be absent or subtle, especially early. A physical examination and laboratory testing answer different questions."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Should I test for Chlamydia psittaci before buying a parrot?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "It is worth discussing with a veterinarian, especially if the bird has respiratory signs, came from a high-risk source, or will live with people at higher risk. Testing is not a substitute for hygiene and veterinary advice."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does a positive result mean the bird must be euthanized?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. A laboratory result is not a treatment or euthanasia decision. A veterinarian should interpret it with the bird's condition, the pathogen, and the risks to other birds and people."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I test a bird myself at home?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Sample collection may be possible, but the value of a molecular test depends on the right sample, timing, transport, and laboratory quality control. A home test kit is not equivalent to a validated laboratory workflow."}}]};
